// Recall-mode questions — Physics & Math
// Format: {s: tag, q: stem, choices: [4], ans: 0-3 index into choices, exp: explanation}
const QB_PHYSICS = [
  {s:'Motion, Forces & Energy',q:'_____ are physical quantities that have both magnitude and direction',choices:['Tensors','Scalars','Matrices','Vectors'],ans:3,exp:'**Vectors** (displacement, velocity, acceleration, force) carry both a size and a direction, so they add tip-to-tail rather than arithmetically. Scalars such as distance, speed and mass have magnitude only.'},
  {s:'Motion, Forces & Energy',q:'_____ are quantities that have only a magnitude',choices:['Matrices','Tensors','Scalars','Vectors'],ans:2,exp:'**Scalars** (distance, speed, mass, energy, temperature) are fully described by a number and a unit. Vectors need a direction as well, which is why speed is a scalar but velocity is a vector.'},
  {s:'Motion, Forces & Energy',q:'For vector addition, use the _____ method, or break the vectors into x and y components and find the resultant magnitude with the _____',choices:['head-to-head / Pythagorean theorem','tail-to-tail / Law of Cosines','tip-to-tail / Pythagorean theorem','tip-to-tail / right-hand rule'],ans:2,exp:'Graphically, place each vector\'s tail at the previous vector\'s tip (**tip-to-tail**); algebraically, sum the x and y components and use the **Pythagorean theorem**, R = √(Rx² + Ry²), with direction θ = tan⁻¹(Ry/Rx). The right-hand rule gives the direction of a cross product, not a vector sum.'},
  {s:'Motion, Forces & Energy',q:'For vector subtraction, you must reverse the _____ of the subtracted vector and then do a tip-to-tail addition',choices:['magnitude','direction','units','dimensions'],ans:1,exp:'A − B = A + (−B), and −B has the same magnitude as B but points the opposite **direction**. The magnitude and units stay the same; only the direction flips.'},
  {s:'Motion, Forces & Energy',q:'Free body diagrams are representations of the _____ acting on an object',choices:['velocities','energies','masses','forces'],ans:3,exp:'A free body diagram isolates one object and draws every **force** on it (gravity, normal, friction, tension) as an arrow. Summing those arrows gives the net force, which sets the acceleration through F = ma.'},
  {s:'Motion, Forces & Energy',q:'_____ occurs in the absence of any net forces acting on an object',choices:['Rotational equilibrium','Thermal equilibrium','Chemical equilibrium','Translational equilibrium'],ans:3,exp:'**Translational equilibrium** means ΣF = 0, so the object has zero acceleration: it is either at rest or moving at constant velocity. Rotational equilibrium is the separate condition Στ = 0.'},
  {s:'Motion, Forces & Energy',q:'_____ occurs in the absence of any net torques acting on an object',choices:['Thermal equilibrium','Translational equilibrium','Rotational equilibrium','Chemical equilibrium'],ans:2,exp:'**Rotational equilibrium** means Στ = 0, so there is no angular acceleration and the object either does not rotate or rotates at constant angular velocity. Translational equilibrium (ΣF = 0) is the force condition, and an object can satisfy one without the other.'},
  {s:'Motion, Forces & Energy',q:'Displacement depends only on _____',choices:['the initial and final positions','the total path length traveled','the average speed along the path','the time elapsed during the motion'],ans:0,exp:'Displacement is the straight-line vector from start to finish, so it is **path independent**: it depends only on the initial and final positions. The total path length is distance, which is path dependent.'},
  {s:'Motion, Forces & Energy',q:'Distance traveled depends on _____',choices:['only the initial and final positions','only the net change in position','the full path taken between two points','only the direction of motion'],ans:2,exp:'Distance is a scalar that adds up the length of every segment of the **path**, so it is path dependent. A runner who completes one lap of a track covers 400 m of distance but has zero displacement.'},
  {s:'Motion, Forces & Energy',q:'Velocity is a _____ and includes both _____ and _____',choices:['scalar / magnitude / direction','vector / magnitude / direction','vector / magnitude / time','scalar / distance / time'],ans:1,exp:'Velocity is a **vector**, the rate of change of displacement (v = Δx/Δt), so it has a **magnitude** (the speed) and a **direction**. Speed alone is the scalar magnitude of velocity.'},
  {s:'Motion, Forces & Energy',q:'Speed is a scalar that gives only the _____ of velocity',choices:['magnitude','direction','rate of change','horizontal component'],ans:0,exp:'Speed is the **magnitude** of velocity, with no direction. The rate of change of velocity is acceleration, a different quantity.'},
  {s:'Motion, Forces & Energy',q:'A force is any push or pull that can cause _____',choices:['an acceleration','a constant velocity','a change in mass','a change in charge'],ans:0,exp:'By Newton\'s second law, a net force produces an **acceleration** (a = F_net/m). A constant velocity is what happens when the net force is zero, which is the common misconception that motion requires a force.'},
  {s:'Motion, Forces & Energy',q:'_____ is the attractive force between two objects as a result of their masses',choices:['Tension','Friction','Gravity','Normal force'],ans:2,exp:'**Gravity** follows F = Gm₁m₂/r², so it depends on both masses and falls off with the square of the distance between their centers. Near Earth\'s surface this reduces to weight, F = mg.'},
  {s:'Motion, Forces & Energy',q:'_____ is a force that opposes motion as a function of electrostatic interactions at the surfaces between two objects',choices:['Normal force','Tension','Gravity','Friction'],ans:3,exp:'**Friction** comes from electrostatic interactions between contacting surfaces and acts opposite to motion (or attempted motion). Static friction (f ≤ μsN) usually exceeds kinetic friction (f = μkN), which is why starting an object sliding is harder than keeping it sliding.'},
  {s:'Motion, Forces & Energy',q:'_____ is a measure of the inertia of an object; its amount of material',choices:['Mass','Weight','Volume','Density'],ans:0,exp:'**Mass** (kg) measures inertia, an object\'s resistance to acceleration, and does not change with location. Weight (mg) is a force and changes with the local gravitational field.'},
  {s:'Motion, Forces & Energy',q:'_____ is the force experienced by a given mass due to the gravitational attraction to the Earth',choices:['Volume','Weight','Density','Mass'],ans:1,exp:'**Weight** is a force, W = mg, measured in newtons. The same object has the same mass on the Moon but about one-sixth of its Earth weight.'},
  {s:'Motion, Forces & Energy',q:'_____ is the vector representation of the change in velocity over time',choices:['Velocity','Displacement','Speed','Acceleration'],ans:3,exp:'**Acceleration** is a = Δv/Δt, in m/s². Because velocity is a vector, changing only the direction (as in uniform circular motion) is still an acceleration.'},
  {s:'Motion, Forces & Energy',q:'_____ is the rotational analog of force; it equals rF sin θ and produces angular acceleration',choices:['Impulse','Torque','Work','Angular momentum'],ans:1,exp:'**Torque** (τ = rF sin θ, in N·m) is greatest when the force acts perpendicular to the lever arm. Work also has units of N·m, but it is energy transferred along a displacement and does not cause rotation.'},
  {s:'Motion, Forces & Energy',q:'Newton\'s first law states that _____',choices:['an object keeps a constant velocity unless acted on by a net external force','an object needs a constant net force to keep moving at a constant velocity','every force is paired with an equal and opposite force on a second object','the net force on an object equals its mass times its acceleration (F = ma)'],ans:0,exp:'Newton\'s first law, the **law of inertia**, says that with zero net force an object\'s velocity (including zero velocity) does not change. The idea that steady motion needs a steady force is the classic misconception this law corrects.'},
  {s:'Motion, Forces & Energy',q:'Newton\'s second law states that _____',choices:['an object keeps a constant velocity unless acted on by a net external force','every force is paired with an equal and opposite force on a second object','the net force on an object equals its mass times its velocity (F = mv)','the net force on an object equals its mass times its acceleration (F = ma)'],ans:3,exp:'Newton\'s second law, **F_net = ma**, means any acceleration is caused by a net force. Mass times velocity is momentum, not force; force is the rate of change of momentum (F = Δp/Δt).'},
  {s:'Motion, Forces & Energy',q:'Newton\'s third law states that _____',choices:['every force is paired with an equal and opposite force on a second object','action and reaction forces act on the same object and cancel out','an object keeps a constant velocity unless acted on by a net external force','the net force on an object equals its mass times its acceleration (F = ma)'],ans:0,exp:'Newton\'s third law: if A pushes on B, then B pushes back on A with an **equal and opposite** force. The two forces act on different objects, so they never cancel each other on a free body diagram.'},
  {s:'Motion, Forces & Energy',q:'_____ is the energy of motion',choices:['Kinetic energy','Chemical energy','Thermal energy','Potential energy'],ans:0,exp:'**Kinetic energy** is K = ½mv², so doubling speed quadruples K. Potential energy is stored energy that depends on position or configuration.'},
  {s:'Motion, Forces & Energy',q:'_____ is a type of energy an object has because of its position or configuration',choices:['Kinetic energy','Chemical energy','Thermal energy','Potential energy'],ans:3,exp:'**Potential energy** is stored energy of position or configuration, such as height in a gravitational field (mgh) or the compression of a spring (½kx²). Kinetic energy depends on motion, not position.'},
  {s:'Motion, Forces & Energy',q:'_____ is the energy an object has because of its height above a reference level (U = mgh)',choices:['Electrical potential energy','Chemical potential energy','Gravitational potential energy','Elastic potential energy'],ans:2,exp:'**Gravitational potential energy** near Earth\'s surface is U = mgh, where h is measured from a chosen zero level. Only changes in U matter physically, so the choice of reference level is arbitrary.'},
  {s:'Motion, Forces & Energy',q:'_____ is stored when a spring is stretched or compressed from its equilibrium length (U = ½kx²)',choices:['Electrical potential energy','Elastic potential energy','Gravitational potential energy','Chemical potential energy'],ans:1,exp:'**Elastic potential energy** is U = ½kx², where k is the spring constant and x is the displacement from equilibrium. It comes from Hooke\'s law, F = −kx.'},
  {s:'Motion, Forces & Energy',q:'_____ is the energy between two charged particles',choices:['Gravitational potential energy','Chemical potential energy','Electrical potential energy','Elastic potential energy'],ans:2,exp:'**Electrical potential energy** is U = kq₁q₂/r. It is negative for opposite charges (an attractive, bound pair) and positive for like charges.'},
  {s:'Motion, Forces & Energy',q:'_____ is the energy stored in the bonds of compounds',choices:['Electrical potential energy','Elastic potential energy','Gravitational potential energy','Chemical potential energy'],ans:3,exp:'**Chemical potential energy** is stored in the arrangement of atoms and electrons in bonds. Note that breaking bonds always requires energy; energy is released only when the new bonds formed are stronger than the ones broken (as in ATP hydrolysis).'},
  {s:'Motion, Forces & Energy',q:'The work done by a conservative force depends only on _____',choices:['the total path length','the initial and final positions','the speed of the object','the time taken'],ans:1,exp:'Conservative forces (gravity, spring and electrostatic forces) are **path independent**: the work they do depends only on the initial and final positions, and the work around any closed loop is zero. That is why they can be described by a potential energy.'},
  {s:'Motion, Forces & Energy',q:'The work done by a non-conservative force such as friction depends on _____',choices:['only the initial and final positions','only the change in height','the path taken','only the mass of the object'],ans:2,exp:'Non-conservative forces such as friction and air resistance are **path dependent**: a longer path dissipates more mechanical energy as thermal energy. Only conservative forces depend solely on the start and end positions.'},
  {s:'Motion, Forces & Energy',q:'_____ is the transfer of energy by a force acting through a displacement (W = Fd cos θ)',choices:['Work','Power','Heat','Impulse'],ans:0,exp:'**Work** (W = Fd cos θ, in joules) is how a force transfers energy, and the work–energy theorem says W_net = ΔK. Heat is energy transfer driven by a temperature difference, and impulse (FΔt) changes momentum, not energy.'},
  {s:'Motion, Forces & Energy',q:'_____ is the rate at which work is done or energy is transferred',choices:['Energy','Power','Force','Work'],ans:1,exp:'**Power** is P = W/t (equivalently P = Fv), measured in watts (1 W = 1 J/s). Work is the energy transferred; power is how fast it is transferred.'},
  {s:'Motion, Forces & Energy',q:'_____ is when systems have the same average kinetic energy and thus the same temperature',choices:['Thermal equilibrium','Static equilibrium','Dynamic equilibrium','Chemical equilibrium'],ans:0,exp:'In **thermal equilibrium** there is no net heat flow between systems because they are at the same temperature. This is the basis of the zeroth law of thermodynamics and of how thermometers work.'},
  {s:'Motion, Forces & Energy',q:'_____ is a measure of the average kinetic energy of the particles that make up a substance',choices:['Temperature','Heat','Entropy','Enthalpy'],ans:0,exp:'**Temperature** is proportional to the average kinetic energy of the particles (for an ideal gas, KE_avg = (3/2)kT). Heat is the energy transferred because of a temperature difference, not a property a system has.'},
  {s:'Motion, Forces & Energy',q:'_____ do not exchange matter or energy with surroundings',choices:['Closed systems','Adiabatic systems','Isolated systems','Open systems'],ans:2,exp:'**Isolated systems** exchange neither matter nor energy, so their total energy is constant (the universe is the classic example). An adiabatic system blocks only heat; it can still exchange energy as work.'},
  {s:'Motion, Forces & Energy',q:'_____ will exchange energy but not matter with their surroundings',choices:['Isolated systems','Open systems','Closed systems','Adiabatic systems'],ans:2,exp:'**Closed systems** can exchange energy (heat and work) but not matter, like a sealed piston. Open systems exchange both, and isolated systems exchange neither.'},
  {s:'Motion, Forces & Energy',q:'_____ will exchange both energy and matter with their surroundings',choices:['Isolated systems','Closed systems','Adiabatic systems','Open systems'],ans:3,exp:'**Open systems** exchange both matter and energy; a living cell or an uncovered pot of boiling water are examples. A closed system exchanges energy only.'},
  {s:'Motion, Forces & Energy',q:'A state function is a property that depends only on the _____ of a system',choices:['history of the system','present state','pathway taken','initial conditions'],ans:1,exp:'State functions (P, V, T, U, H, S, G) depend only on the **present state** of the system, so a change in them depends only on the initial and final states. Work and heat are not state functions because they depend on the pathway.'},
  {s:'Motion, Forces & Energy',q:'A process function describes the _____ from one equilibrium state to another',choices:['pathway','equilibrium state','present state','final state'],ans:0,exp:'Process (path) functions such as work and heat depend on the **pathway** taken between states. For example, the work done by a gas equals the area under its P–V curve, which differs for different paths.'},
  {s:'Motion, Forces & Energy',q:'_____ is energy transfer between two objects at different temperatures',choices:['Work','Heat','Energy','Power'],ans:1,exp:'**Heat** (q) always flows spontaneously from the hotter object to the colder one, by conduction, convection or radiation, until thermal equilibrium is reached. Work is energy transfer by a force through a displacement.'},
  {s:'Motion, Forces & Energy',q:'Specific heat is the amount of energy needed to _____',choices:['raise the temperature of 1 g of a substance by 1 °C','raise the temperature of 1 g of a substance by 10 °C','raise the temperature of a whole object by 1 °C','melt 1 g of a substance at its melting point'],ans:0,exp:'**Specific heat** (c) is the energy needed to raise 1 g of a substance by 1 °C (1 K), used in q = mcΔT; water\'s c is 1 cal/(g·°C), or 4.184 J/(g·K). The energy to heat a whole object by 1 °C is its heat capacity, and melting energy is the heat of fusion.'},
  {s:'Motion, Forces & Energy',q:'_____ is the amount of energy per unit mass required for a phase change',choices:['Heat capacity','Specific heat','Heat of transformation','Enthalpy of reaction'],ans:2,exp:'The **heat of transformation** (latent heat, L) gives q = mL; temperature stays constant during the phase change. Specific heat (q = mcΔT) applies only while the temperature is changing within one phase.'},
  {s:'Motion, Forces & Energy',q:'Isobaric processes have constant _____',choices:['volume','temperature','pressure','entropy'],ans:2,exp:'**Isobaric** means constant **pressure** (baros = weight); on a P–V diagram it is a horizontal line, and the work done is W = PΔV.'},
  {s:'Motion, Forces & Energy',q:'Isothermal processes have constant _____',choices:['entropy','pressure','volume','temperature'],ans:3,exp:'**Isothermal** means constant **temperature**, so for an ideal gas ΔU = 0 and Q = W. On a P–V diagram it is a hyperbola (PV = constant).'},
  {s:'Motion, Forces & Energy',q:'No _____ is exchanged in an adiabatic process',choices:['volume','heat','pressure','work'],ans:1,exp:'In an **adiabatic** process Q = 0, so by the first law ΔU = −W (sign convention W = work done by the system): expansion cools the gas and compression warms it. Work can still be done; only heat exchange is zero.'},
  {s:'Motion, Forces & Energy',q:'Isovolumetric processes have constant _____',choices:['pressure','volume','entropy','temperature'],ans:1,exp:'In an **isovolumetric** (isochoric) process the **volume** is constant, so no P–V work is done (W = 0) and ΔU = Q. On a P–V diagram it is a vertical line.'},
  {s:'Motion, Forces & Energy',q:'_____ is a measure of how much energy has spread out',choices:['Gibbs free energy','Entropy','Internal energy','Enthalpy'],ans:1,exp:'**Entropy** (S) measures how dispersed energy is (ΔS = Q_rev/T). By the second law, the entropy of an isolated system never decreases in a spontaneous process.'},
  {s:'Fluids',q:'_____ are substances that flow and conform to the shape of their containers',choices:['Fluids','Solids','Crystalline solids','Rigid bodies'],ans:0,exp:'**Fluids** (liquids and gases) cannot withstand shear stress, so they flow and take the shape of their container. Solids, crystalline or not, resist shear and keep their own shape.'},
  {s:'Fluids',q:'_____ maintain their shape regardless of their container',choices:['Liquids','Gases','Solids','Fluids'],ans:2,exp:'**Solids** have fixed particle positions and can resist shear forces, so they keep their own shape. Liquids keep their volume but not their shape, and gases keep neither.'},
  {s:'Fluids',q:'In a static fluid, pressure at a given depth is _____',choices:['equal in every direction','greatest in the downward direction','greatest in the upward direction','zero in the horizontal direction'],ans:0,exp:'Pressure is a scalar: at any point in a static fluid it acts **equally in all directions**, and it depends only on depth (P = P₀ + ρgh), not on the shape of the container. Assuming pressure only pushes downward is the common error.'},
  {s:'Fluids',q:'_____ is the sum of all pressures at a certain point within a fluid',choices:['Gauge pressure','Atmospheric pressure','Hydrostatic pressure','Absolute pressure'],ans:3,exp:'**Absolute pressure** is P = P₀ + ρgh: the pressure at the surface (usually atmospheric) plus the pressure of the fluid column above the point. Gauge pressure leaves out the atmospheric part.'},
  {s:'Fluids',q:'In water, every additional 10 meters of depth adds approximately _____ to the total pressure',choices:['0.1 atm','2 atm','0.5 atm','1 atm'],ans:3,exp:'ρgh = (1000 kg/m³)(9.8 m/s²)(10 m) ≈ 98,000 Pa ≈ **1 atm** (1 atm = 101,325 Pa). A diver at 10 m therefore feels about 2 atm of absolute pressure.'},
  {s:'Fluids',q:'Gauge pressure is _____ minus _____',choices:['absolute pressure / atmospheric pressure','atmospheric pressure / absolute pressure','absolute pressure / hydrostatic pressure','hydrostatic pressure / absolute pressure'],ans:0,exp:'**P_gauge = P_abs − P_atm**, the pressure above atmospheric that a tire gauge or blood pressure cuff reads. For an open container it equals ρgh; reversing the order gives a negative value.'},
  {s:'Fluids',q:'Pascal\'s principle states that pressure applied to an enclosed fluid will be distributed _____ throughout the entire volume of the fluid',choices:['undiminished','doubled','inversely proportional to distance','reduced by half'],ans:0,exp:'By **Pascal\'s principle**, a pressure change applied to an enclosed incompressible fluid is transmitted **undiminished** to every part of it. This is the basis of hydraulic lifts and brakes.'},
  {s:'Fluids',q:'Hydraulic machines operate based on Pascal\'s principle to generate _____',choices:['mechanical advantage','turbulent flow','viscous drag','frictional force'],ans:0,exp:'Equal pressure on both pistons means F₁/A₁ = F₂/A₂, so a small force on a small piston gives a large force on a large piston (**mechanical advantage**). Energy is conserved: the small piston must move a proportionally longer distance (W = F₁d₁ = F₂d₂).'},
  {s:'Fluids',q:'Archimedes\' principle states that the buoyant force equals the _____',choices:['density of the object','weight of the fluid displaced by the object','volume of the object','weight of the object itself'],ans:1,exp:'**Archimedes\' principle**: F_B = ρ_fluid·V_submerged·g, the **weight of the displaced fluid**. The buoyant force equals the object\'s own weight only when the object floats.'},
  {s:'Fluids',q:'Specific gravity is the ratio of an object\'s density to _____',choices:['the density of water','the mass of the object','the density of air','the density of mercury'],ans:0,exp:'**Specific gravity** = ρ_object/ρ_water (ρ_water = 1 g/cm³ = 1000 kg/m³), so it is unitless. An object with specific gravity below 1 floats in water, and the fraction submerged equals its specific gravity.'},
  {s:'Fluids',q:'Cohesion refers to the clinging of _____ molecules. Adhesion refers to the clinging of _____ molecules',choices:['unlike / like','like / like','like / unlike','polar / nonpolar'],ans:2,exp:'**Cohesion** is attraction between **like** molecules (water to water); **adhesion** is attraction between **unlike** molecules (water to glass). Cohesion produces surface tension, and adhesion stronger than cohesion produces a concave meniscus.'},
  {s:'Fluids',q:'Capillary action occurs when the _____ to the wall are stronger than the _____ between the liquid molecules',choices:['cohesive forces / adhesive forces','buoyant forces / cohesive forces','viscous forces / adhesive forces','adhesive forces / cohesive forces'],ans:3,exp:'When **adhesive forces** (liquid to wall) exceed **cohesive forces** (liquid to liquid), the liquid climbs the narrow tube and forms a concave meniscus, as water does in glass. Mercury has stronger cohesion than adhesion, so it is depressed and forms a convex meniscus.'},
  {s:'Fluids',q:'Surface tension is created by _____',choices:['gravitational forces','buoyant forces','adhesive forces','cohesive forces'],ans:3,exp:'Molecules at the surface have no neighbors above them, so **cohesive forces** pull them inward and the surface behaves like a stretched film. This is why water striders can walk on water; adhesive forces govern how a liquid wets a surface instead.'},
  {s:'Fluids',q:'_____ is a measure of a fluid\'s internal friction',choices:['Density','Viscosity','Capillary action','Surface tension'],ans:1,exp:'**Viscosity** (η) is a fluid\'s resistance to flow from internal friction between layers; honey has high viscosity. Higher viscosity lowers flow rate in Poiseuille\'s law and favors laminar flow by lowering the Reynolds number.'},
  {s:'Fluids',q:'_____ is smooth flow in parallel layers with no mixing between them',choices:['Turbulent flow','Pulsatile flow','Unsteady flow','Laminar flow'],ans:3,exp:'In **laminar flow**, fluid moves in smooth parallel layers, fastest at the center of a tube and slowest at the walls. It occurs at low Reynolds numbers, and Poiseuille\'s law applies only to laminar flow.'},
  {s:'Fluids',q:'_____ is rough, disorderly flow with eddies that develops above a critical speed',choices:['Steady flow','Plug flow','Turbulent flow','Laminar flow'],ans:2,exp:'**Turbulent flow** has chaotic eddies and occurs at high Reynolds numbers, above a critical speed. In medicine, turbulence past a narrowed valve or artery causes the sounds heard as murmurs and bruits.'},
  {s:'Fluids',q:'Poiseuille\'s law determines the _____ of laminar flow through a tube',choices:['Reynolds number','volumetric flow rate','surface tension','buoyant force'],ans:1,exp:'**Poiseuille\'s law**: Q = πr⁴ΔP/(8ηL), so the **volumetric flow rate** depends on the fourth power of radius. Halving a vessel\'s radius cuts flow 16-fold at the same pressure difference, which is why arteriolar tone controls blood flow so strongly.'},
  {s:'Fluids',q:'Volumetric flow rate is the volume of fluid which passes per _____',choices:['unit time','unit area','unit mass','unit volume'],ans:0,exp:'Volumetric flow rate is Q = V/t = Av (m³/s), the volume passing a point per **unit time**. For an incompressible fluid Q is the same everywhere in a closed pipe, which gives the continuity equation A₁v₁ = A₂v₂.'},
  {s:'Fluids',q:'Narrow passages cause fluid to flow _____ and wider passages cause flow to be _____',choices:['slow / fast','fast / fast','fast / slow','slow / slow'],ans:2,exp:'By the continuity equation (A₁v₁ = A₂v₂), the same flow rate through a smaller area requires a higher speed, so flow is **fast** in narrow passages and **slow** in wide ones. Think of putting your thumb over a garden hose.'},
  {s:'Fluids',q:'According to the Venturi effect, the velocity of a fluid in a constricted area will _____ and its static pressure will _____',choices:['decrease / increase','increase / increase','decrease / decrease','increase / decrease'],ans:3,exp:'In a constriction the velocity **increases** (continuity), and by Bernoulli\'s equation (P + ½ρv² + ρgh = constant) the static pressure **decreases**. The mistake is to assume squeezing a fluid raises its pressure.'},
  {s:'Fluids',q:'As the cross-sectional area of a Venturi tube decreases, the linear speed must _____',choices:['decrease','stay the same','increase','drop to zero'],ans:2,exp:'For an incompressible fluid, Q = Av is constant, so a smaller area A forces a larger speed v. The faster flow in the narrow section has lower pressure, which a Venturi meter uses to measure flow.'},
  {s:'Fluids',q:'In a closed fluid system, as the total cross-sectional area increases, the velocity _____',choices:['drops to zero','increases','stays the same','decreases'],ans:3,exp:'Because Q = Av is constant, a larger total area means a lower velocity. That is why blood moves slowest in the capillaries, which have the greatest total cross-sectional area, allowing time for exchange.'},
  {s:'Fluids',q:'Laminar flow occurs at _____ Reynolds numbers',choices:['low','high','negative','undefined'],ans:0,exp:'The Reynolds number (Re = ρvd/η) compares inertial to viscous forces; at **low** Re viscous forces dominate and keep flow laminar, while at high Re flow becomes turbulent. In a pipe, flow is typically laminar below about Re = 2000.'},
  {s:'Fluids',q:'The Reynolds number is used to check whether flow is _____ or _____',choices:['steady / unsteady','compressible / incompressible','viscous / inviscid','laminar / turbulent'],ans:3,exp:'The **Reynolds number** (ratio of inertial to viscous forces) predicts whether flow will be **laminar** (low Re) or **turbulent** (high Re). It rises with fluid density, speed and tube diameter and falls with viscosity.'},
  {s:'Fluids',q:'Fluids at rest can exert _____ forces but not shear forces',choices:['oblique','parallel','tangential','perpendicular'],ans:3,exp:'A static fluid cannot sustain shear stress, so the force it exerts on any surface is **perpendicular** (normal) to that surface. Parallel and tangential forces are shear forces, which only moving viscous fluids exert.'},
  {s:'Electricity & Magnetism',q:'The _____ is the unit of charge',choices:['ampere','ohm','coulomb','volt'],ans:2,exp:'Charge is measured in **coulombs** (C); one electron carries e = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C. The ampere is a rate of charge flow (1 A = 1 C/s), not a unit of charge.'},
  {s:'Electricity & Magnetism',q:'Protons have a _____ charge and electrons have a _____ charge',choices:['negative / positive','positive / neutral','positive / negative','neutral / negative'],ans:2,exp:'Protons carry +e and electrons carry −e, equal in magnitude (1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C), even though a proton is about 1836 times more massive. Neutrons are neutral.'},
  {s:'Electricity & Magnetism',q:'Opposite charges exert _____ forces and like charges exert _____ forces',choices:['repulsive / attractive','attractive / attractive','repulsive / repulsive','attractive / repulsive'],ans:3,exp:'Opposite charges **attract** and like charges **repel**. In Coulomb\'s law (F = kq₁q₂/r²), a negative product of charges means attraction and a positive product means repulsion.'},
  {s:'Electricity & Magnetism',q:'_____ are materials that allow charge to flow freely',choices:['Dielectrics','Conductors','Insulators','Semiconductors'],ans:1,exp:'**Conductors** such as metals have delocalized electrons that move freely, and in electrostatic equilibrium any excess charge sits on their outer surface. Semiconductors conduct only partially, and dielectrics are insulators.'},
  {s:'Electricity & Magnetism',q:'_____ are materials that resist the movement of charge',choices:['Semiconductors','Electrolytes','Conductors','Insulators'],ans:3,exp:'In **insulators** (glass, rubber, plastic) electrons are tightly bound, so charge placed on them stays where it is put. Insulators used between capacitor plates are called dielectrics.'},
  {s:'Electricity & Magnetism',q:'Coulomb\'s law gives the magnitude of the _____ between two charges',choices:['magnetic force','nuclear force','gravitational force','electrostatic force'],ans:3,exp:'Coulomb\'s law, F = kq₁q₂/r², gives the **electrostatic force**, which falls off with the square of the distance just as gravity does. Doubling r cuts the force to one-quarter.'},
  {s:'Electricity & Magnetism',q:'The Coulomb\'s law proportionality constant is k = _____',choices:['6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ N·m²/kg²','8.85 × 10⁻¹² C²/(N·m²)','1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C','8.99 × 10⁹ N·m²/C²'],ans:3,exp:'**k = 8.99 × 10⁹ N·m²/C²** (about 9 × 10⁹), so that F = kq₁q₂/r² comes out in newtons. k = 1/(4πε₀), where ε₀ = 8.85 × 10⁻¹² C²/(N·m²) is the permittivity of free space; 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ is the gravitational constant G.'},
  {s:'Electricity & Magnetism',q:'Every charge generates a(n) _____ which can exert forces on other charges',choices:['electric field','gravitational field','magnetic field','nuclear field'],ans:0,exp:'A charge Q creates an **electric field** E = kQ/r², and a charge q placed in it feels F = qE. A magnetic field is produced only by moving charges, not by a stationary one.'},
  {s:'Electricity & Magnetism',q:'Field lines show the direction of the force on a _____ test charge',choices:['neutral','positive','negative','moving'],ans:1,exp:'By convention, field lines show the force on a **positive** test charge, so they point away from positive charges and toward negative ones. A negative charge feels a force opposite to the field lines.'},
  {s:'Electricity & Magnetism',q:'An equipotential line is a line on which the _____',choices:['charge is zero everywhere','potential is the same at every point','force on a charge is maximal','electric field is zero everywhere'],ans:1,exp:'On an equipotential line the **potential is the same** everywhere, so no work is done moving a charge along it. Equipotential lines are always perpendicular to electric field lines; the field itself is generally not zero there.'},
  {s:'Electricity & Magnetism',q:'An electric dipole is a _____',choices:['separation of positive and negative charges','flow of electrons','alignment of magnetic domains','movement of protons'],ans:0,exp:'An **electric dipole** is a pair of equal and opposite charges separated by a distance, as in a polar molecule such as water. In a uniform field a dipole feels a torque that aligns it with the field but no net force.'},
  {s:'Electricity & Magnetism',q:'A dipole moment is a measure of the _____ of positive and negative electrical charges within a system',choices:['separation','oscillation','rotation','vibration'],ans:0,exp:'The dipole moment is p = qd, charge times **separation** distance (units C·m). By convention in physics it points from the negative to the positive charge, while chemistry arrows are often drawn the opposite way.'},
  {s:'Electricity & Magnetism',q:'_____ is the amount of work required to bring a test charge from infinitely far away to a given position',choices:['Elastic potential energy','Chemical potential energy','Electrical potential energy','Gravitational potential energy'],ans:2,exp:'**Electrical potential energy** is U = kQq/r, the work needed to bring charge q from infinity (where U = 0) to a distance r from Q. Dividing by q gives the electric potential, V = kQ/r.'},
  {s:'Electricity & Magnetism',q:'Electric potential energy _____ when like charges move closer or opposite charges move apart',choices:['doubles','stays the same','increases','decreases'],ans:2,exp:'Both moves work against the electrostatic force, so external work is stored and U **increases**. In U = kq₁q₂/r, a positive product (like charges) grows as r shrinks, and a negative product (opposite charges) becomes less negative as r grows.'},
  {s:'Electricity & Magnetism',q:'Electric potential energy _____ when like charges move apart or opposite charges move closer',choices:['increases','halves','decreases','stays the same'],ans:2,exp:'Both moves go with the electrostatic force, so the field does positive work and U **decreases**, converting to kinetic energy. This is the same reason a dropped ball loses gravitational potential energy.'},
  {s:'Electricity & Magnetism',q:'_____ is the potential energy per unit charge at a point in an electric field',choices:['Electric potential energy','Electric potential','Electric flux','Electric field'],ans:1,exp:'**Electric potential** is V = U/q = kQ/r, measured in volts (J/C); it is a scalar. Electric potential energy (U = qV) depends on the size of the test charge, while the potential does not.'},
  {s:'Electricity & Magnetism',q:'_____ is the difference in electric potential between two points in an electric field',choices:['Current','Power','Resistance','Voltage'],ans:3,exp:'**Voltage** (potential difference, ΔV = W/q) is the work per unit charge needed to move a charge between two points, in volts (J/C). It is a difference in potential energy per charge, not a difference in charge.'},
  {s:'Electricity & Magnetism',q:'Test charges move spontaneously in the direction that results in _____ in electrical potential energy',choices:['a doubling','no change','a decrease','an increase'],ans:2,exp:'Like a ball rolling downhill, charges move spontaneously to **decrease** their potential energy. Positive charges therefore move toward lower potential, and negative charges move toward higher potential, yet both lose potential energy.'},
  {s:'Electricity & Magnetism',q:'A magnetic field is a region around a magnetic material or moving electric charge within which the _____',choices:['electric field exists','force of magnetism acts','charge flows','force of gravity acts'],ans:1,exp:'A **magnetic field** (B, in tesla) is where magnetic forces act; it is produced by moving charges or magnetic materials. It exerts a force only on moving charges, F = qvB sin θ.'},
  {s:'Electricity & Magnetism',q:'_____ possess no unpaired electrons and are slightly repelled by a magnet',choices:['Superconducting materials','Diamagnetic materials','Ferromagnetic materials','Paramagnetic materials'],ans:1,exp:'**Diamagnetic** materials have all electrons paired, so they have no net magnetic moment and are weakly repelled by a magnetic field (mnemonic: dia = "die away"). Paramagnetic materials have unpaired electrons and are weakly attracted.'},
  {s:'Electricity & Magnetism',q:'_____ possess some unpaired electrons and become weakly magnetic in an external magnetic field',choices:['Superconducting materials','Ferromagnetic materials','Diamagnetic materials','Paramagnetic materials'],ans:3,exp:'**Paramagnetic** materials (such as O₂ and aluminum) have unpaired electrons whose moments align with an external field, so they are weakly attracted but lose their magnetism when the field is removed. Ferromagnetic materials are strongly attracted and can stay magnetized.'},
  {s:'Electricity & Magnetism',q:'_____ possess some unpaired electrons and become strongly magnetic in an external magnetic field',choices:['Superconducting materials','Paramagnetic materials','Ferromagnetic materials','Diamagnetic materials'],ans:2,exp:'**Ferromagnetic** materials (iron, nickel, cobalt) have unpaired electrons grouped into aligned magnetic domains, so they become strongly magnetic and can stay magnetized below the Curie temperature. Paramagnetic materials are only weakly attracted.'},
  {s:'Electricity & Magnetism',q:'Current-carrying wires create magnetic fields that are _____ surrounding the wire',choices:['concentric circles','radial lines','helical spirals','parallel lines'],ans:0,exp:'A straight wire makes a field of **concentric circles** around it, with magnitude B = μ₀I/(2πr). Use the right-hand rule: thumb along the current, and the fingers curl in the direction of B.'},
  {s:'Electricity & Magnetism',q:'The _____ is the sum of the electrostatic and magnetic forces acting on a charged particle',choices:['Lorentz force','nuclear force','gravitational force','Coulomb force'],ans:0,exp:'The **Lorentz force** is F = qE + qv × B, combining the electric and magnetic forces. The Coulomb force is only the electrostatic part.'},
  {s:'Electricity & Magnetism',q:'_____ is the flow of electric charge',choices:['Resistance','Current','Voltage','Power'],ans:1,exp:'**Current** is I = Q/t, measured in amperes (C/s). By convention it is the direction positive charge would flow, which is opposite to the actual drift of electrons in a wire.'},
  {s:'Electricity & Magnetism',q:'_____ is the flow of current due to movement of electrons',choices:['Semiconductor conduction','Superconduction','Electrolytic conduction','Metallic conduction'],ans:3,exp:'In **metallic conduction**, delocalized electrons in a metal lattice carry the current. Electrolytic conduction instead depends on ions moving through a solution, as in the body\'s fluids.'},
  {s:'Electricity & Magnetism',q:'_____ is the movement of free ions under an electric field',choices:['Metallic conduction','Superconduction','Semiconductor conduction','Electrolytic conduction'],ans:3,exp:'In **electrolytic conduction**, dissolved cations and anions carry current, so conductivity rises with ion concentration. That is why pure water conducts poorly while salt water conducts well.'},
  {s:'Electricity & Magnetism',q:'The junction rule states that the sum of currents flowing into a junction is _____ the sum flowing out',choices:['proportional to','greater than','equal to','less than'],ans:2,exp:'Kirchhoff\'s **junction rule** (ΣI_in = ΣI_out) follows from conservation of charge: charge cannot pile up at a node. It is why current splits among parallel branches but adds back to the same total.'},
  {s:'Electricity & Magnetism',q:'The loop rule states that the sum of all electric potential differences around a closed loop is _____',choices:['the total current','the total resistance','zero','the largest voltage drop'],ans:2,exp:'Kirchhoff\'s **loop rule** follows from conservation of energy: the voltage gained across sources equals the voltage dropped across resistors, so the signed sum around any closed loop is **zero**.'},
  {s:'Electricity & Magnetism',q:'_____ is the opposition that a substance offers to the flow of electrons',choices:['Conductance','Capacitance','Resistance','Inductance'],ans:2,exp:'**Resistance** is R = ρL/A (in ohms): it rises with resistivity and length and falls with cross-sectional area, and it appears in Ohm\'s law V = IR. Conductance is its reciprocal (1/R).'},
  {s:'Electricity & Magnetism',q:'_____ are conductive materials with moderate resistance that slow electrons without stopping them',choices:['Capacitors','Resistors','Diodes','Inductors'],ans:1,exp:'**Resistors** dissipate energy as heat (P = I²R). In series their resistances add (R = R₁ + R₂); in parallel, 1/R = 1/R₁ + 1/R₂, so the total is less than the smallest resistor.'},
  {s:'Electricity & Magnetism',q:'_____ store charge and electrical potential energy in an electric field and can discharge it',choices:['Resistors','Capacitors','Diodes','Inductors'],ans:1,exp:'**Capacitors** store charge Q = CV on two separated plates, holding energy U = ½CV². A defibrillator works by discharging a charged capacitor; inductors store energy in a magnetic field instead.'},
  {s:'Electricity & Magnetism',q:'_____ are insulators placed between the plates of a capacitor that increase capacitance',choices:['Magnetic materials','Dielectric materials','Superconducting materials','Conducting materials'],ans:1,exp:'A **dielectric** with constant κ multiplies capacitance (C = κε₀A/d) by partly canceling the field between the plates. A conductor between the plates would short the capacitor.'},
  {s:'Electricity & Magnetism',q:'Ammeters are inserted in series and measure _____',choices:['voltage','power','resistance','current'],ans:3,exp:'An **ammeter** measures **current** and goes in series so the same current passes through it; an ideal ammeter has zero resistance so it does not change the current it measures.'},
  {s:'Electricity & Magnetism',q:'Voltmeters are inserted in parallel to measure _____',choices:['power','voltage','current','resistance'],ans:1,exp:'A **voltmeter** measures the potential difference across a component, so it goes in parallel; an ideal voltmeter has infinite resistance so it draws no current from the circuit.'},
  {s:'Electricity & Magnetism',q:'Ohmmeters are used to measure _____',choices:['capacitance','current','resistance','voltage'],ans:2,exp:'An **ohmmeter** measures **resistance** by applying its own small known voltage and measuring the resulting current (R = V/I), so the component must be disconnected from other power sources.'},
  {s:'Waves, Sound & Light',q:'_____ is the maximum displacement of a wave from its rest position',choices:['Frequency','Amplitude','Wavelength','Period'],ans:1,exp:'**Amplitude** is the maximum displacement from equilibrium. A wave\'s energy and intensity are proportional to amplitude squared, while frequency and wavelength are independent of amplitude.'},
  {s:'Waves, Sound & Light',q:'_____ is the distance between two crests or two troughs of a wave',choices:['Period','Amplitude','Frequency','Wavelength'],ans:3,exp:'**Wavelength** (λ, in meters) is the length of one full cycle, crest to crest. It is linked to speed and frequency by v = fλ.'},
  {s:'Waves, Sound & Light',q:'_____ is the number of cycles a wave makes per second',choices:['Amplitude','Period','Frequency','Wavelength'],ans:2,exp:'**Frequency** (f) is cycles per second, in hertz (Hz = s⁻¹). It is the reciprocal of period (f = 1/T), and it does not change when a wave passes into a new medium.'},
  {s:'Waves, Sound & Light',q:'_____ is the number of seconds it takes to complete a cycle',choices:['Frequency','Amplitude','Period','Wavelength'],ans:2,exp:'The **period** (T) is the time for one cycle, T = 1/f. A 50 Hz wave has a period of 0.02 s.'},
  {s:'Waves, Sound & Light',q:'_____ refers to how far a point on a wave is from the equilibrium position at a given moment',choices:['Displacement','Acceleration','Velocity','Amplitude'],ans:0,exp:'**Displacement** (x or y) is the instantaneous distance of a point from equilibrium and changes throughout the cycle. Amplitude is the maximum value of that displacement.'},
  {s:'Waves, Sound & Light',q:'Angular frequency measures _____ per _____',choices:['linear displacement / unit time','unit time / angular displacement','angular displacement / unit time','wavelength / unit time'],ans:2,exp:'Angular frequency is **angular displacement per unit time**, ω = 2πf = 2π/T, in radians per second. One full cycle is 2π radians, which is why the factor 2π appears.'},
  {s:'Waves, Sound & Light',q:'_____ are oscillations where particles are displaced perpendicular to the wave direction',choices:['Longitudinal waves','Surface waves','Standing waves','Transverse waves'],ans:3,exp:'In **transverse waves** (a plucked string, electromagnetic waves), the oscillation is perpendicular to the direction of travel. Only transverse waves can be polarized.'},
  {s:'Waves, Sound & Light',q:'_____ are oscillations where particles are displaced parallel to the wave direction',choices:['Surface waves','Standing waves','Transverse waves','Longitudinal waves'],ans:3,exp:'In **longitudinal waves** (sound), particles oscillate along the direction of travel, forming compressions and rarefactions. Transverse waves oscillate perpendicular to travel instead.'},
  {s:'Waves, Sound & Light',q:'Light is a _____',choices:['longitudinal wave','surface wave','transverse wave','standing wave'],ans:2,exp:'Light is a **transverse wave**: its electric and magnetic fields oscillate perpendicular to the direction it travels. That is why light can be polarized and sound cannot.'},
  {s:'Waves, Sound & Light',q:'Sound waves are _____',choices:['standing waves','longitudinal waves','transverse waves','surface waves'],ans:1,exp:'Sound is a mechanical **longitudinal wave** of compressions and rarefactions in a medium. It needs a medium, so it cannot travel through a vacuum, unlike light.'},
  {s:'Waves, Sound & Light',q:'_____ describes the ways in which waves interact in space to form a resultant wave',choices:['Diffraction','Reflection','Interference','Refraction'],ans:2,exp:'**Interference** follows the principle of superposition: overlapping waves add their displacements point by point. Diffraction is the spreading of a single wave around an obstacle or through an opening.'},
  {s:'Waves, Sound & Light',q:'_____ occurs when waves are exactly in phase with each other',choices:['Resonance','Partial interference','Destructive interference','Constructive interference'],ans:3,exp:'With a phase difference of 0 (or a whole number of wavelengths), crests line up with crests and amplitudes add: **constructive interference**. Two identical in-phase waves give double the amplitude.'},
  {s:'Waves, Sound & Light',q:'_____ occurs when waves are exactly out of phase with each other',choices:['Resonance','Partial interference','Destructive interference','Constructive interference'],ans:2,exp:'With a phase difference of 180° (half a wavelength), crests meet troughs and amplitudes subtract: **destructive interference**. Noise-canceling headphones use this principle.'},
  {s:'Waves, Sound & Light',q:'_____ have continuously shifting points of maximum and minimum displacement',choices:['Surface waves','Traveling waves','Longitudinal waves','Standing waves'],ans:1,exp:'In **traveling waves** the crests and troughs move through space, carrying energy along. In standing waves the nodes and antinodes stay fixed in place.'},
  {s:'Waves, Sound & Light',q:'_____ oscillate in time but the peak amplitude profile does not move in space',choices:['Standing waves','Longitudinal waves','Surface waves','Traveling waves'],ans:0,exp:'**Standing waves** form when two waves of the same frequency travel in opposite directions and interfere, as on a string fixed at both ends. Their nodes and antinodes stay at fixed positions.'},
  {s:'Waves, Sound & Light',q:'Nodes on a standing wave are points that have _____',choices:['zero displacement at all times','maximum displacement','maximum oscillation amplitude','continuously shifting displacement'],ans:0,exp:'A **node** never moves because the two interfering waves always cancel there, so it has zero displacement at all times. Antinodes, halfway between nodes, have the maximum amplitude.'},
  {s:'Waves, Sound & Light',q:'On a standing wave, _____ are points of maximum oscillation',choices:['nodes','troughs','antinodes','crests'],ans:2,exp:'**Antinodes** oscillate with the largest amplitude and lie halfway between nodes. For a string fixed at both ends, the ends are nodes and λ = 2L/n.'},
  {s:'Waves, Sound & Light',q:'_____ is the large increase in amplitude that occurs when a periodic force is applied at an object\'s natural frequency',choices:['Diffraction','Interference','Resonance','Damping'],ans:2,exp:'**Resonance** occurs when the driving frequency matches the natural frequency, so energy builds up and the amplitude grows, as when a singer shatters a glass. Damping does the opposite and reduces amplitude.'},
  {s:'Waves, Sound & Light',q:'Damping is a decrease in _____',choices:['wavelength','period','amplitude','frequency'],ans:2,exp:'**Damping** removes energy from an oscillating system (usually as heat through friction or drag), so the **amplitude** decreases over time. Shock absorbers in a car are designed to damp oscillations.'},
  {s:'Waves, Sound & Light',q:'Sound is produced when something _____',choices:['vibrates','diffracts','is polarized','is refracted'],ans:0,exp:'Sound is a mechanical longitudinal wave produced when an object **vibrates** and pushes on the surrounding medium, creating compressions and rarefactions. Sound cannot be polarized because it is longitudinal.'},
  {s:'Waves, Sound & Light',q:'Sound propagates through all matter but not through _____',choices:['gases','solids','a vacuum','water'],ans:2,exp:'Sound is a mechanical wave that needs particles to pass the vibration along, so it cannot travel through **a vacuum**. Light, an electromagnetic wave, can.'},
  {s:'Waves, Sound & Light',q:'Pitch is our perception of _____',choices:['frequency','wavelength','amplitude','period'],ans:0,exp:'**Pitch** is the perceived **frequency** of a sound: higher frequency means higher pitch. Loudness is the perception of intensity, which depends on amplitude.'},
  {s:'Waves, Sound & Light',q:'_____ is the power transmitted per unit area by a sound wave; it increases with amplitude and is perceived as loudness',choices:['Timbre','Pitch','Frequency','Intensity'],ans:3,exp:'**Intensity** (I = P/A, in W/m²) is proportional to amplitude squared and is perceived as loudness on the logarithmic decibel scale, β = 10 log(I/I₀); each 10 dB is a 10-fold increase in intensity. Pitch is the perception of frequency, and timbre is sound quality.'},
  {s:'Waves, Sound & Light',q:'The Doppler effect is a shift in the perceived _____ when the source and detector move relative to one another',choices:['frequency','amplitude','wave speed','intensity at the source'],ans:0,exp:'The **Doppler effect** changes the perceived **frequency**: f\' = f(v ± v_D)/(v ∓ v_S). The speed of sound in the medium does not change; only how often wavefronts reach the detector does.'},
  {s:'Waves, Sound & Light',q:'By the Doppler effect, the perceived frequency is _____ when the source and detector move toward each other',choices:['unchanged','lower','unpredictable','higher'],ans:3,exp:'Moving closer compresses the wavefronts, so the perceived frequency is **higher**, as with the rising pitch of an approaching siren. In the Doppler equation, use the top signs (+ in the numerator, − in the denominator) for "toward".'},
  {s:'Waves, Sound & Light',q:'By the Doppler effect, the perceived frequency is _____ when the source and detector move away from each other',choices:['lower','unpredictable','unchanged','higher'],ans:0,exp:'Moving apart stretches the wavefronts, so the perceived frequency is **lower**, as with the falling pitch of a siren that has passed. In astronomy the same effect for light is called redshift.'},
  {s:'Waves, Sound & Light',q:'When the source and detector move in the same direction, the apparent frequency depends on _____',choices:['only the speed of the source','only the speed of the detector','the speeds of both the source and the detector','only the wavelength emitted'],ans:2,exp:'In f\' = f(v ± v_D)/(v ∓ v_S), **both** velocities appear, so the shift depends on how fast each one moves. If they move at the same velocity, there is no shift; if the source gains on the detector, the frequency rises.'},
  {s:'Waves, Sound & Light',q:'Ultrasound uses high-frequency sound waves to compare _____ of tissues in the body',choices:['relative densities','absolute densities','temperatures','chemical compositions'],ans:0,exp:'Ultrasound reflects at boundaries between tissues of different density (more precisely, acoustic impedance), so the echoes map **relative densities**. Doppler ultrasound uses the frequency shift of echoes from moving blood to measure flow.'},
  {s:'Waves, Sound & Light',q:'Arrange the media by speed of sound: fastest _____, middle _____, slowest _____',choices:['gas / liquid / solid','solid / liquid / gas','liquid / solid / gas','gas / solid / liquid'],ans:1,exp:'Sound travels fastest in **solids**, then **liquids**, then **gases**, because v = √(B/ρ) and stiffness (bulk modulus B) rises much more than density from gas to solid. In air sound travels at about 343 m/s, in water about 1500 m/s.'},
  {s:'Waves, Sound & Light',q:'Electromagnetic waves consist of an oscillating _____ and an oscillating _____',choices:['electric field / gravitational field','magnetic field / nuclear field','electric field / magnetic field','gravitational field / nuclear field'],ans:2,exp:'An electromagnetic wave is made of an oscillating **electric field** and **magnetic field** that are perpendicular to each other and to the direction of travel. It needs no medium and travels at c = 3 × 10⁸ m/s in a vacuum.'},
  {s:'Waves, Sound & Light',q:'The electromagnetic spectrum is the range of _____ and _____ found in electromagnetic waves',choices:['velocities / amplitudes','amplitudes / phases','masses / charges','wavelengths / frequencies'],ans:3,exp:'The EM spectrum spans all **wavelengths** and their matching **frequencies** (c = fλ), from radio waves to gamma rays. All EM waves travel at the same speed c in a vacuum, so speed does not vary across the spectrum.'},
  {s:'Waves, Sound & Light',q:'In the visible spectrum, _____ has the longest wavelength',choices:['violet','green','blue','red'],ans:3,exp:'**Red** light has the longest visible wavelength (about 700 nm) and the lowest photon energy. Mnemonic ROY G BIV runs from long to short wavelength.'},
  {s:'Waves, Sound & Light',q:'In the visible spectrum, _____ has the shortest wavelength',choices:['yellow','violet','red','orange'],ans:1,exp:'**Violet** light has the shortest visible wavelength (about 400 nm) and the highest photon energy (E = hc/λ). Just beyond violet lies ultraviolet.'},
  {s:'Waves, Sound & Light',q:'Light will be refracted when passing between media with different _____',choices:['indices of refraction','densities','chemical compositions','temperatures'],ans:0,exp:'Refraction happens because light changes speed between media with different **indices of refraction**, following Snell\'s law: n₁ sin θ₁ = n₂ sin θ₂. Light bends toward the normal when it enters a medium with a higher n.'},
  {s:'Waves, Sound & Light',q:'_____ is a dimensionless number describing how fast light propagates through a material',choices:['Specific gravity','Reynolds number','Dielectric constant','Index of refraction'],ans:3,exp:'The **index of refraction** is n = c/v, so it is always at least 1 (vacuum n = 1, water about 1.33, glass about 1.5). A larger n means light travels more slowly in that material.'},
  {s:'Waves, Sound & Light',q:'The Rydberg formula is used to predict the _____ of light emitted or absorbed after an electron moves between energy levels',choices:['wavelength','intensity','phase','amplitude'],ans:0,exp:'The **Rydberg formula**, 1/λ = R(1/n₁² − 1/n₂²), gives the **wavelength** of the photon for a hydrogen electron transition. Larger energy gaps give shorter wavelengths.'},
  {s:'Waves, Sound & Light',q:'Diffraction is the bending and spreading of light waves as they pass through a _____',choices:['narrow slit','flat mirror','wide aperture','curved surface'],ans:0,exp:'**Diffraction** is strongest when the opening is about the size of the wavelength, so light spreads noticeably only through a **narrow slit**. Through a wide aperture the spreading is negligible.'},
  {s:'Waves, Sound & Light',q:'Young\'s double-slit experiment shows the _____ and _____ interference of waves',choices:['linear / circular','longitudinal / transverse','constructive / destructive','reflective / refractive'],ans:2,exp:'In Young\'s experiment, bright fringes form where light from the two slits arrives in phase (**constructive** interference) and dark fringes where it arrives out of phase (**destructive** interference). This demonstrated the wave nature of light.'},
  {s:'Waves, Sound & Light',q:'_____ is light whose electric field oscillates along only one direction',choices:['Circularly polarized light','Plane-polarized light','Unpolarized light','Coherent light'],ans:1,exp:'In **plane-polarized** (linearly polarized) light the electric field vibrates in a single plane, which a polarizing filter produces from unpolarized light. In circularly polarized light the field direction rotates as the wave travels.'},
  {s:'Waves, Sound & Light',q:'_____ is the rebounding of incident light waves at a medium\'s boundary',choices:['Dispersion','Reflection','Refraction','Diffraction'],ans:1,exp:'**Reflection** sends light back into the original medium, with the angle of reflection equal to the angle of incidence. Refraction is the bending of light that passes into the new medium.'},
  {s:'Waves, Sound & Light',q:'The law of reflection states that the angle of reflection _____ the angle of incidence',choices:['is complementary to','is equal to','is greater than','is less than'],ans:1,exp:'The **law of reflection**: θ_reflection = θ_incidence, both measured from the normal (the line perpendicular to the surface). Measuring from the surface instead of the normal is a common error.'},
  {s:'Waves, Sound & Light',q:'For a concave mirror, if the object is farther than the focal point, the image will be _____ and _____',choices:['virtual / upright','real / upright','real / inverted','virtual / inverted'],ans:2,exp:'A concave (converging) mirror forms a **real, inverted** image when the object is beyond the focal point; real images are always inverted for a single mirror or lens. Moving the object inside the focal point gives a virtual, upright, magnified image instead.'},
  {s:'Waves, Sound & Light',q:'For a concave mirror, if the object is inside the focal point, the image will be _____ and _____',choices:['real / inverted','real / upright','virtual / inverted','virtual / upright'],ans:3,exp:'With the object inside the focal point, a concave mirror forms a **virtual, upright**, magnified image behind the mirror, which is how a makeup or shaving mirror works. Beyond the focal point the image becomes real and inverted.'},
  {s:'Waves, Sound & Light',q:'For a convex mirror, the image will always be _____ and _____',choices:['virtual / upright','real / inverted','real / upright','virtual / inverted'],ans:0,exp:'A convex (diverging) mirror always forms a **virtual, upright**, reduced image, whatever the object position, which is why car side mirrors warn that objects are closer than they appear. Diverging mirrors and lenses can never form real images of real objects.'},
  {s:'Waves, Sound & Light',q:'_____ is the bending of light as it passes from one medium to another',choices:['Dispersion','Refraction','Diffraction','Reflection'],ans:1,exp:'**Refraction** is caused by the change in light\'s speed between media and follows Snell\'s law (n₁ sin θ₁ = n₂ sin θ₂). Diffraction is bending around obstacles or through openings, not across a boundary.'},
  {s:'Waves, Sound & Light',q:'_____ is when various wavelengths of light separate from each other',choices:['Dispersion','Refraction','Diffraction','Reflection'],ans:0,exp:'**Dispersion** happens because the index of refraction varies slightly with wavelength (violet bends more than red), so a prism splits white light into a spectrum. It is also the cause of chromatic aberration in lenses.'},
  {s:'Waves, Sound & Light',q:'_____ is when light cannot be refracted out of a medium and is reflected back inside',choices:['Chromatic dispersion','Single-slit diffraction','Plane polarization','Total internal reflection'],ans:3,exp:'**Total internal reflection** occurs only when light goes from a higher to a lower index of refraction at an angle greater than the critical angle. Fiber optics and endoscopes use it to carry light along a fiber.'},
  {s:'Waves, Sound & Light',q:'The critical angle is the angle of incidence that produces an angle of refraction of exactly _____ degrees',choices:['180','0','90','45'],ans:2,exp:'At the critical angle (sin θc = n₂/n₁, only when n₁ > n₂), the refracted ray skims along the boundary at **90°** from the normal. At any larger angle of incidence, total internal reflection occurs.'},
  {s:'Waves, Sound & Light',q:'A convex lens is also known as a _____ lens',choices:['diverging','scattering','reflecting','converging'],ans:3,exp:'A convex lens is thicker in the middle and bends parallel rays to a real focal point, so it is a **converging** lens with a positive focal length. It is used to correct hyperopia (farsightedness).'},
  {s:'Waves, Sound & Light',q:'A concave lens is also known as a _____ lens',choices:['converging','diverging','scattering','reflecting'],ans:1,exp:'A concave lens is thinner in the middle and spreads parallel rays apart, so it is a **diverging** lens with a negative focal length. It is used to correct myopia (nearsightedness).'},
  {s:'Waves, Sound & Light',q:'For an object outside the focal point of a convex lens, the image is _____ and _____',choices:['virtual / upright','real / upright','virtual / inverted','real / inverted'],ans:3,exp:'A converging lens forms a **real, inverted** image on the far side of the lens when the object is beyond the focal point, as in a camera or the eye. From the thin lens equation (1/f = 1/o + 1/i), i is positive here.'},
  {s:'Waves, Sound & Light',q:'For an object inside the focal point of a convex lens, the image is _____ and _____',choices:['virtual / upright','real / inverted','real / upright','virtual / inverted'],ans:0,exp:'With the object inside f, a converging lens forms a **virtual, upright**, magnified image on the same side as the object; this is how a magnifying glass works. The image distance i comes out negative in 1/f = 1/o + 1/i.'},
  {s:'Waves, Sound & Light',q:'Images formed by a concave lens are always _____ and _____',choices:['real / inverted','virtual / upright','real / upright','virtual / inverted'],ans:1,exp:'A concave (diverging) lens always forms a **virtual, upright**, reduced image on the same side as the object. For a single mirror or lens, virtual images are upright and real images are inverted.'},
  {s:'Nuclear & Atomic Physics',q:'The _____ is the ejection of an electron from the surface of a metal in response to light',choices:['Zeeman effect','Stark effect','Compton effect','photoelectric effect'],ans:3,exp:'In the **photoelectric effect**, a photon with energy above the work function ejects an electron with K_max = hf − W. It showed that light comes in quanta (photons), because brighter light below the threshold frequency ejects no electrons.'},
  {s:'Nuclear & Atomic Physics',q:'The work function is the minimum energy necessary to _____',choices:['eject an electron from a given metal surface','excite an electron within an isolated atom','split a nucleus into two smaller nuclei','raise the temperature of a metal by 1 °C'],ans:0,exp:'The **work function** (W = hf₀) is the minimum energy needed to free an electron from a given metal\'s surface. Any extra photon energy becomes the electron\'s kinetic energy: K_max = hf − W.'},
  {s:'Nuclear & Atomic Physics',q:'The threshold frequency is the minimum light frequency necessary to _____',choices:['raise the temperature of a metal by 1 °C','excite an electron within an isolated atom','split a nucleus into two smaller nuclei','eject an electron from a given metal surface'],ans:3,exp:'The **threshold frequency** f₀ = W/h is the lowest frequency that can eject an electron from a given metal. Below f₀, no electrons are emitted no matter how intense the light, because each photon alone lacks enough energy.'},
  {s:'Nuclear & Atomic Physics',q:'If an electron absorbs a photon, its principal quantum number n will _____',choices:['increase','decrease','stay the same','become zero'],ans:0,exp:'Absorbing a photon of exactly the right energy (ΔE = hf) moves the electron to a higher energy level, so n **increases**. Dropping back to a lower level emits a photon of the same energy difference.'},
  {s:'Nuclear & Atomic Physics',q:'When an electron falls from a higher to a lower energy orbit, it _____',choices:['emits a photon of light','absorbs a photon','releases a neutrino','emits an electron'],ans:0,exp:'An electron that drops to a lower level **emits a photon** whose energy equals the gap between levels (E = hf = hc/λ). This produces the bright lines of an emission spectrum.'},
  {s:'Nuclear & Atomic Physics',q:'The emission and absorption spectra of a substance tell us _____',choices:['what elements are found in the substance','the temperature of the substance','the density of the substance','the mass of the substance'],ans:0,exp:'Each element has a unique set of energy levels, so its line spectrum acts as a fingerprint that identifies **which elements are present**. Emission lines and absorption lines occur at the same wavelengths for a given element.'},
  {s:'Nuclear & Atomic Physics',q:'_____ is the near-instantaneous emission of longer-wavelength light by a substance that has just absorbed light, stopping as soon as the excitation source is removed',choices:['Luminescence','Phosphorescence','Fluorescence','Incandescence'],ans:2,exp:'In **fluorescence**, an excited electron loses a little energy without radiating and then emits a lower-energy, longer-wavelength photon within nanoseconds. Phosphorescence is delayed emission that continues after excitation stops, and luminescence is the general umbrella term.'},
  {s:'Nuclear & Atomic Physics',q:'_____ occurs when small nuclei combine into larger nuclei',choices:['Radioactive decay','Nuclear fusion','Nuclear fission','Electron capture'],ans:1,exp:'**Fusion** joins light nuclei (such as hydrogen into helium in the Sun) and releases energy because the product has a higher binding energy per nucleon. The released energy comes from the mass defect, E = mc².'},
  {s:'Nuclear & Atomic Physics',q:'_____ occurs when a large nucleus splits into smaller nuclei',choices:['Nuclear fission','Nuclear fusion','Electron capture','Radioactive decay'],ans:0,exp:'**Fission** splits a heavy nucleus (such as uranium-235 after it absorbs a neutron) into lighter nuclei and releases neutrons and energy. It powers nuclear reactors through a chain reaction.'},
  {s:'Nuclear & Atomic Physics',q:'_____ is when an unstable atomic nucleus loses energy by emitting radiation',choices:['Radioactive decay','Nuclear fusion','Neutron absorption','Nuclear binding'],ans:0,exp:'**Radioactive decay** is the spontaneous emission of radiation (alpha, beta or gamma) by an unstable nucleus as it moves toward a more stable state. It follows first-order kinetics with a characteristic half-life.'},
  {s:'Nuclear & Atomic Physics',q:'Alpha decay is the emission of an alpha particle, which is a _____ nucleus',choices:['beryllium','hydrogen','lithium','helium'],ans:3,exp:'An alpha particle is a **helium-4** nucleus (2 protons, 2 neutrons), so alpha decay lowers the mass number by 4 and the atomic number by 2. Alpha particles are the most massive and least penetrating type of radiation.'},
  {s:'Nuclear & Atomic Physics',q:'Beta-minus decay is the conversion of a _____ into a _____',choices:['proton / neutron','neutron / proton','electron / positron','positron / electron'],ans:1,exp:'In beta-minus decay a **neutron** becomes a **proton**, emitting an electron and an antineutrino, so Z increases by 1 and A is unchanged. It happens in nuclei with too many neutrons.'},
  {s:'Nuclear & Atomic Physics',q:'Beta-plus decay is the conversion of a _____ into a _____',choices:['neutron / proton','electron / positron','proton / neutron','neutron / electron'],ans:2,exp:'In beta-plus (positron) decay a **proton** becomes a **neutron**, emitting a positron and a neutrino, so Z decreases by 1 and A is unchanged. It happens in proton-rich nuclei; electron capture has the same net effect on Z.'},
  {s:'Nuclear & Atomic Physics',q:'Gamma decay is the emission of a _____ ray made up of _____',choices:['beta / electrons','alpha / neutrons','positron / neutrinos','gamma / high-energy photons'],ans:3,exp:'In gamma decay an excited nucleus drops to a lower energy state by emitting a **gamma ray** of **high-energy photons**. Because photons have no mass or charge, neither A nor Z changes.'},
  {s:'Nuclear & Atomic Physics',q:'_____ is a process unstable atoms use to become more stable by drawing an electron into the nucleus',choices:['Alpha decay','Electron capture','Beta decay','Gamma decay'],ans:1,exp:'In **electron capture**, the nucleus absorbs an inner-shell electron, which combines with a proton to form a neutron, so Z decreases by 1 and A is unchanged. Beta-minus decay instead emits an electron and raises Z.'},
  {s:'Nuclear & Atomic Physics',q:'_____ is the amount of time required for half of a sample of radioactive nuclei to decay',choices:['Decay constant','Mean lifetime','Half-life','Doubling time'],ans:2,exp:'The **half-life** is t½ = ln 2/λ ≈ 0.693/λ. After n half-lives, (1/2)ⁿ of the sample remains; for example, 1/8 remains after 3 half-lives.'},
  {s:'Nuclear & Atomic Physics',q:'A plot of the number of undecayed radioactive nuclei versus time shows _____ decay',choices:['exponential','logarithmic','linear','quadratic'],ans:0,exp:'Radioactive decay is first order, N = N₀e^(−λt), so the curve is an **exponential** decay that drops by half every half-life and approaches zero without reaching it. On a plot of ln N versus time, the same data form a straight line with slope −λ.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'x = log_b(y) can also be written as _____',choices:['b = x^y','x = y^b','y = b^x','b = y^x'],ans:2,exp:'A logarithm is an exponent: log_b(y) = x means **b raised to x gives y** (y = b^x). For example, log₂(8) = 3 because 2³ = 8.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'x = ln(y) can also be written as _____',choices:['x = y^e','y = e^x','e = y^x','e = x^y'],ans:1,exp:'The natural log has base e (≈ 2.718), so ln(y) = x means **y = e^x**. To convert to base 10, ln(y) ≈ 2.303 log(y).'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'log_b(1) = _____',choices:['-1','0','1','undefined'],ans:1,exp:'Any base raised to the power **0** equals 1 (b⁰ = 1), so log_b(1) = 0. The log of 0 is undefined, which is a different case.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'log_b(b) = _____',choices:['1','undefined','0','-1'],ans:0,exp:'b¹ = b, so log_b(b) = **1**; for example, log(10) = 1 and ln(e) = 1.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'log(A × B) = _____',choices:['log(A) - log(B)','log(A) + log(B)','log(A) × log(B)','log(A)/log(B)'],ans:1,exp:'The log of a product is the **sum** of the logs, because multiplying powers adds exponents. This is why each 10-fold increase in [H⁺] changes pH by exactly 1.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'log(A/B) = _____',choices:['log(A) × log(B)','log(A) + log(B)','log(A) - log(B)','log(A)/log(B)'],ans:2,exp:'The log of a quotient is the **difference** of the logs, log(A) − log(B), because dividing powers subtracts exponents. Dividing the logs, log(A)/log(B), is a different (change-of-base) operation.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'log(A^B) = _____',choices:['log(A) + log(B)','B × log(A)','A × log(B)','log(A)/log(B)'],ans:1,exp:'The power rule brings the exponent to the front: log(A^B) = **B × log(A)**. For example, log(10³) = 3 × log(10) = 3.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'log(1/A) = _____',choices:['-log(A)','1/log(A)','log(-A)','log(A) - 1'],ans:0,exp:'log(1/A) = log(1) − log(A) = 0 − log(A) = **−log(A)**. This is why pH = −log[H⁺] can also be written log(1/[H⁺]).'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'For 1 ≤ A < 10, log(A × 10^B) is approximately _____',choices:['A + 0.B','B + 0.A','log(B) + A','B × 0.A'],ans:1,exp:'log(A × 10^B) = B + log(A), and since log(A) lies between 0 and 1 it is roughly **0.A**, giving the MCAT shortcut B + 0.A. For example, −log(2 × 10⁻⁵) ≈ 5 − 0.2 = 4.8 (exact value 4.7); the shortcut is least accurate for middle values of A.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'In scientific notation multiplication, multiply the coefficients and then _____',choices:['subtract the exponents','divide the exponents','multiply the exponents','add the exponents'],ans:3,exp:'(a × 10^m)(b × 10^n) = ab × 10^(m+n), so you **add the exponents**. For example, (2 × 10³)(3 × 10⁴) = 6 × 10⁷.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'In scientific notation division, divide the coefficients and then _____',choices:['divide the exponents','add the exponents','multiply the exponents','subtract the exponents'],ans:3,exp:'(a × 10^m)/(b × 10^n) = (a/b) × 10^(m−n), so you **subtract the exponents**. For example, (8 × 10⁶)/(2 × 10²) = 4 × 10⁴.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'To raise a number in scientific notation to a power, raise the coefficient to the power and then _____',choices:['divide the exponents','multiply the exponents','subtract the exponents','add the exponents'],ans:1,exp:'(a × 10^m)^n = aⁿ × 10^(mn), so you **multiply the exponents**. For example, (3 × 10⁴)² = 9 × 10⁸; adding the exponents is the common slip.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'To take the square root of a number in scientific notation, take the square root of the coefficient and then _____',choices:['add 2 to the exponent','subtract 2 from the exponent','multiply the exponent by 2','divide the exponent by 2'],ans:3,exp:'√(a × 10^m) = √a × 10^(m/2), so you **divide the exponent by 2**. First make the exponent even: √(4 × 10⁷) = √(40 × 10⁶) ≈ 6.3 × 10³.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'The LARS mnemonic for adjusting scientific notation stands for _____',choices:['Left Multiply, Right Divide','Left Subtract, Right Add','Left Add, Right Subtract','Left Divide, Right Multiply'],ans:2,exp:'**LARS: Left Add, Right Subtract.** Moving the decimal point left one place adds 1 to the exponent, and moving it right subtracts 1; for example, 450 × 10² = 4.5 × 10⁴.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'On the unit circle, x = _____ and y = _____',choices:['sin θ / cos θ','cos θ / sin θ','tan θ / cos θ','sin θ / tan θ'],ans:1,exp:'A point on the unit circle at angle θ has coordinates (**cos θ**, **sin θ**), since the radius (the hypotenuse) is 1. The slope of that radius, y/x, is tan θ.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'cos(0°) = _____ and sin(0°) = _____',choices:['0 / 1','1 / 1','1 / 0','0 / 0'],ans:2,exp:'At 0° the point on the unit circle is (1, 0), so cos 0° = **1** and sin 0° = **0**. In physics, this means a force at 0° to the displacement does its full value of work (W = Fd cos 0°).'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'cos(30°) ≈ _____ and sin(30°) = _____',choices:['0.5 / 0.9','0.7 / 0.7','1 / 0','0.9 / 0.5'],ans:3,exp:'cos 30° = √3/2 ≈ **0.87 (about 0.9)** and sin 30° = **0.5** exactly. A useful check: at small angles sine is the smaller value, and the values swap at 60°.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'cos(45°) ≈ _____ and sin(45°) ≈ _____',choices:['0.7 / 0.7','0.5 / 0.9','0.9 / 0.5','1 / 0'],ans:0,exp:'At 45° the two legs are equal, so cos 45° = sin 45° = √2/2 ≈ **0.71**. That is why the x and y components of a vector at 45° are equal.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'cos(60°) = _____ and sin(60°) ≈ _____',choices:['0.9 / 0.5','0.5 / 0.9','0.7 / 0.7','0 / 1'],ans:1,exp:'cos 60° = **0.5** exactly and sin 60° = √3/2 ≈ **0.87 (about 0.9)**, the reverse of 30°, because sin θ = cos(90° − θ).'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'cos(90°) = _____ and sin(90°) = _____',choices:['1 / 0','1 / 1','0 / 1','0 / 0'],ans:2,exp:'At 90° the point on the unit circle is (0, 1), so cos 90° = **0** and sin 90° = **1**. This is why a force perpendicular to displacement does no work, and why torque (τ = rF sin θ) is greatest at 90°.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'tan(θ) = _____',choices:['cos(θ)/sin(θ)','1/sin(θ)','sin(θ) × cos(θ)','sin(θ)/cos(θ)'],ans:3,exp:'**tan θ = sin θ/cos θ**, which equals opposite/adjacent (SOH-CAH-TOA). The reciprocal, cos θ/sin θ, is cot θ.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'sin(θ) = _____',choices:['opposite/adjacent','hypotenuse/opposite','opposite/hypotenuse','adjacent/hypotenuse'],ans:2,exp:'**SOH**: sine = **opposite/hypotenuse**. Opposite/adjacent is tangent, and adjacent/hypotenuse is cosine.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'cos(θ) = _____',choices:['opposite/hypotenuse','hypotenuse/adjacent','opposite/adjacent','adjacent/hypotenuse'],ans:3,exp:'**CAH**: cosine = **adjacent/hypotenuse**. Hypotenuse/adjacent is the reciprocal, sec θ.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'In order, the steps of the scientific method are: formulate a hypothesis, then _____, then _____',choices:['draw conclusions / test that hypothesis','publish the results / gather background information','test that hypothesis / analyze the data and draw conclusions','assume the hypothesis is true / discard contradicting data'],ans:2,exp:'After forming a testable hypothesis, you **test it** by collecting data and then **analyze the data and draw conclusions**, which may lead to a revised hypothesis. Background research comes before the hypothesis, not after it.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'The FINER mnemonic asks if research is _____, _____, _____, _____ and _____',choices:['valid / reliable / novel / ethical / feasible','objective / measurable / novel / legal / relevant','funded / innovative / necessary / efficient / reproducible','feasible / interesting / novel / ethical / relevant'],ans:3,exp:'**FINER**: a good research question is **Feasible, Interesting, Novel, Ethical and Relevant**. Validity and reliability describe measurements, not whether a question is worth asking.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'The four tenets of medical ethics are _____, _____, _____ and _____',choices:['beneficence / nonmaleficence / autonomy / justice','beneficence / fidelity / autonomy / justice','justice / nonmaleficence / equity / respect','autonomy / veracity / fidelity / confidentiality'],ans:0,exp:'The four principles are **beneficence** (act in the patient\'s best interest), **nonmaleficence** (do no harm), **autonomy** (respect the patient\'s informed choices) and **justice** (distribute care fairly). Veracity, fidelity and confidentiality are important duties but not among the four core tenets.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'Clinical research requires _____, which is genuine uncertainty about which treatment is better',choices:['equipotential','equipoise','equanimity','equivalence'],ans:1,exp:'**Equipoise** makes it ethical to randomize patients: if one treatment were already known to be better, assigning anyone to the worse arm would be unethical. A trial should be stopped early once one arm is clearly superior or harmful.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'_____ occurs in a lab and does not include human subjects',choices:['Translational research','Epidemiological research','Clinical research','Basic science research'],ans:3,exp:'**Basic science research** studies mechanisms in cells, tissues or animals under controlled lab conditions. It gives the most control over variables but the least direct generalizability to patients.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'_____ connects basic science research to clinical research',choices:['Epidemiological research','Basic science research','Clinical research','Translational research'],ans:3,exp:'**Translational research** moves findings "from bench to bedside", turning lab discoveries into tests and treatments for patients. Clinical research studies human subjects directly.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'The _____ is the variable that is changed or controlled in an experiment',choices:['dependent variable','moderating variable','confounding variable','independent variable'],ans:3,exp:'The **independent variable** is the one the researcher manipulates (the cause), and it is plotted on the x-axis. The dependent variable is the measured outcome.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'The _____ is the outcome variable that a researcher measures',choices:['dependent variable','independent variable','moderating variable','confounding variable'],ans:0,exp:'The **dependent variable** is the measured outcome that may depend on the independent variable, and it is plotted on the y-axis. Mnemonic: the dependent variable depends on the independent one.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'A _____ is a group that receives a treatment known to produce the expected response',choices:['experimental group','negative control','positive control','pilot group'],ans:2,exp:'A **positive control** shows that the assay can detect an effect when one exists, so a negative result in the experimental group can be trusted. A negative control is expected to show no response.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'A _____ is a group in which no response is expected',choices:['experimental group','pilot group','positive control','negative control'],ans:3,exp:'A **negative control** (such as a placebo or vehicle-only group) shows what happens without an effective treatment, revealing background signal or false positives. A positive control is expected to respond.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'_____, also known as _____, is the degree to which measurements are true and accurate',choices:['Validity / accuracy','Reliability / precision','Sensitivity / specificity','Power / significance'],ans:0,exp:'**Validity (accuracy)** is how close a measurement is to the true value. Reliability (precision) is how consistent repeated measurements are; a scale can be precise yet consistently wrong.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'When a scientist gets very similar results upon repetition, the results are said to be _____ or _____',choices:['valid / accurate','reliable / reproducible','significant / powerful','biased / confounded'],ans:1,exp:'Results that agree on repetition are **reliable** (**reproducible**, precise). Reliability does not guarantee validity: tightly clustered darts can all miss the bullseye.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'A _____ is the complete, well-defined group of individuals with shared characteristics that a study wants to describe',choices:['research sample','control group','research population','convenience sample'],ans:2,exp:'The **research population** is the entire group of interest; researchers usually study a sample drawn from it and infer back to it. Random sampling helps make the sample representative of the population.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'A _____ is a group taken from a larger population for measurement',choices:['research sample','control group','research population','reference population'],ans:0,exp:'A **research sample** is the subset actually measured; statistics computed on it estimate the population\'s parameters. A sample that is not representative introduces selection bias.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'_____ refers to how well an experiment avoids confounding variables',choices:['Statistical validity','Internal validity','External validity','Ecological validity'],ans:1,exp:'**Internal validity** is confidence that the independent variable, not a confounder, caused the change in the dependent variable. Randomization, blinding and controls raise it; external validity is about generalizability instead.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'_____ refers to the ability of a study\'s results to be generalized to the population and to other settings',choices:['Statistical validity','Construct validity','Internal validity','External validity'],ans:3,exp:'**External validity** is how well results generalize beyond the study sample and conditions. Tight lab control often raises internal validity at the cost of external validity.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'A _____ exposes all participants to every treatment or condition',choices:['within-subject design','cross-sectional design','factorial design','between-group design'],ans:0,exp:'In a **within-subject** (repeated-measures) design, each participant serves as their own control, which reduces variability between people but risks order and practice effects (handled by counterbalancing). A crossover trial is one type of within-subject design.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'A _____ assigns different groups of participants to different conditions, so each participant experiences only one condition',choices:['factorial design','within-subject design','between-group design','crossover design'],ans:2,exp:'In a **between-group** (between-subjects) design, each group gets a different level of the independent variable and results are compared across groups. A within-subject design exposes everyone to all conditions, and a factorial design manipulates two or more independent variables at once.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'A result has _____ if it is unlikely to have occurred by chance',choices:['effect size','clinical significance','statistical significance','practical significance'],ans:2,exp:'A result is **statistically significant** when p < α (usually 0.05). Statistical significance says nothing about whether the effect is large or matters to patients, which is clinical significance.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'_____ refers to the usefulness of findings to patient care or outcomes',choices:['Clinical significance','Statistical power','Effect size','Statistical significance'],ans:0,exp:'**Clinical significance** asks whether an effect is large enough to change patient care. A very large trial can find a statistically significant but clinically trivial difference, such as a 1 mmHg drop in blood pressure.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'A _____ is an observational study that follows exposed and unexposed groups over time to compare how often an outcome develops',choices:['cohort study','randomized controlled trial','cross-sectional study','case-control study'],ans:0,exp:'A **cohort study** starts from exposure and follows people forward to see who develops the outcome, so it can calculate incidence and relative risk. In a randomized controlled trial the researcher assigns the exposure, so it is experimental, not observational.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'A _____ analyzes data collected at one given point in time across a sample population',choices:['cohort study','randomized controlled trial','case-control study','cross-sectional study'],ans:3,exp:'A **cross-sectional study** is a snapshot that measures exposure and outcome at the same time, so it estimates prevalence. Because it cannot show which came first, it cannot establish temporality or causation.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'A _____ identifies two existing groups differing in outcome and compares them',choices:['cross-sectional study','cohort study','randomized controlled trial','case-control study'],ans:3,exp:'A **case-control study** starts from the outcome (cases with the disease, controls without it) and looks back for past exposures, giving an odds ratio. It is efficient for rare diseases but prone to recall bias.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'Which Bradford Hill criterion is the only one considered necessary to establish causation, requiring that the cause precede the effect?',choices:['Specificity','Temporality','Analogy','Coherence'],ans:1,exp:'The Bradford Hill criteria (strength, consistency, specificity, **temporality**, biological gradient, plausibility, coherence, experiment, analogy) support causal inference, but only temporality, exposure before outcome, is strictly required. Specificity (one cause, one effect) is the weakest criterion, since many exposures cause several diseases.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'_____ is bias introduced when proper randomization is not achieved',choices:['Selection bias','Detection bias','Hawthorne effect','Confirmation bias'],ans:0,exp:'**Selection bias** occurs when the people in the study, or in each group, are not representative, often because assignment was not random. Random sampling and random assignment are the main defenses.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'_____ arises from searching for an outcome disproportionately in certain populations',choices:['Selection bias','Hawthorne effect','Detection bias','Confirmation bias'],ans:2,exp:'**Detection bias** happens when one group is screened or examined more closely, so the outcome is found more often there even if its true rate is the same. For example, a group that sees doctors more often will have more diagnoses recorded.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'The _____ is when subject behavior is altered simply by knowing they are being studied',choices:['Pygmalion effect','placebo effect','Hawthorne effect','nocebo effect'],ans:2,exp:'In the **Hawthorne effect**, participants change their behavior because they know they are being observed. The Pygmalion effect is different: the researcher\'s expectations shape the participants\' performance.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'_____ is the tendency to answer questions in a manner viewed favorably by others',choices:['Social desirability bias','Selection bias','Detection bias','Confirmation bias'],ans:0,exp:'**Social desirability bias** makes participants underreport behaviors such as drinking and overreport behaviors such as exercise. Anonymous surveys help reduce it.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'The _____ occurs when an inactive treatment improves a patient\'s condition because of expectation',choices:['placebo effect','Hawthorne effect','nocebo effect','Pygmalion effect'],ans:0,exp:'In the **placebo effect**, belief that a treatment will help produces real improvement; double-blind, placebo-controlled trials account for it. The nocebo effect is the reverse, where negative expectations cause harm or side effects.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'A _____ influences both the dependent and independent variable, causing a spurious association',choices:['confounding variable','mediating variable','moderating variable','independent variable'],ans:0,exp:'A **confounder** is linked to both exposure and outcome, so it can create a false association; for example, smoking confounds the link between coffee and lung cancer. A mediator, by contrast, lies on the causal pathway between them.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'A _____ lies on the causal pathway and explains how one variable affects another',choices:['mediating variable','confounding variable','independent variable','moderating variable'],ans:0,exp:'A **mediator** carries the effect from X to Y (X → M → Y); for example, exercise lowers blood pressure partly by reducing weight. A moderator changes the strength of the X–Y relationship without lying on the pathway.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'A _____ influences the strength or direction of a relationship between two other variables',choices:['moderating variable','independent variable','confounding variable','mediating variable'],ans:0,exp:'A **moderator** changes how strongly X affects Y, an interaction; for example, a drug may work better in younger patients, so age moderates the effect. A mediator explains how X affects Y.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'The average of a set of numbers is called the _____',choices:['range','mode','mean','median'],ans:2,exp:'The **mean** is the sum of the values divided by n. It is pulled toward outliers, so the median better represents skewed data such as income.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'The _____ is the middle number in an ordered data set',choices:['mean','range','median','mode'],ans:2,exp:'The **median** is the 50th percentile; for an even count, it is the average of the two middle values. Because it ignores extreme values, it resists outliers better than the mean.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'The number which appears most often in a set is called the _____',choices:['median','range','mode','mean'],ans:2,exp:'The **mode** is the most frequent value; a data set can have no mode, one mode, or several (bimodal). It is the only measure of central tendency usable with categorical data.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'A normal distribution has a mean and median that are _____, with _____ of the data within 1 standard deviation of the mean',choices:['unequal / about 95%','equal / about 68%','equal / about 95%','unequal / about 68%'],ans:1,exp:'A normal curve is symmetric, so mean = median = mode, and the **68-95-99.7 rule** applies: about 68% of data within 1 SD, 95% within 2 SD and 99.7% within 3 SD. The 95% figure belongs to 2 SD, not 1.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'A standard normal distribution has a mean of 0 and a _____',choices:['standard deviation of 0','standard deviation of 10','median of 1','standard deviation of 1'],ans:3,exp:'The standard normal (z) distribution has mean 0 and **standard deviation 1** (so variance 1 as well). Any normal value converts to it with z = (x − μ)/σ.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'_____ have a longer tail on one side, which pulls the mean away from the median and mode toward that tail',choices:['Bimodal distributions','Skewed distributions','Uniform distributions','Normal distributions'],ans:1,exp:'In **skewed distributions** the mean is dragged toward the tail, so mean, median and mode differ; the median stays between the mean and the mode. Normal distributions are symmetric, so all three are equal.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'A left-skewed distribution is also known as _____ skewed',choices:['positively','symmetrically','negatively','bimodally'],ans:2,exp:'Skew is named for the direction of the tail: a left tail points toward negative values, so the distribution is **negatively** skewed and the mean < median < mode. A common trap is naming skew by where the peak sits.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'A right-skewed distribution is also known as _____ skewed',choices:['negatively','bimodally','positively','symmetrically'],ans:2,exp:'A long right tail (toward positive values) makes a distribution **positively** skewed, with mean > median > mode. Income distributions are the classic example.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'A _____ is a distribution with two different modes appearing as distinct peaks',choices:['normal distribution','bimodal distribution','uniform distribution','skewed distribution'],ans:1,exp:'A **bimodal distribution** has two peaks, which often means two subgroups are mixed together (such as heights of men and women combined). Its mean can fall in the valley between the peaks, where few data points lie.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'The _____ of a data set is the highest number minus the lowest number',choices:['interquartile range','standard deviation','range','variance'],ans:2,exp:'The **range** = maximum − minimum. Because it uses only the two most extreme values, it is very sensitive to outliers, unlike the interquartile range.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'The _____ is the difference between the 75th and 25th percentiles',choices:['interquartile range','variance','range','standard deviation'],ans:0,exp:'The **interquartile range** (IQR = Q3 − Q1) covers the middle 50% of the data and resists outliers. A common rule flags outliers below Q1 − 1.5 × IQR or above Q3 + 1.5 × IQR.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'The _____ quantifies the spread of data values around the mean in the same units as the data',choices:['interquartile range','standard deviation','range','variance'],ans:1,exp:'The **standard deviation** is the square root of the variance, so it is in the original units of the data. Variance also measures spread around the mean, but in squared units.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'_____ are data points that differ markedly from the other observations',choices:['Modes','Quartiles','Percentiles','Outliers'],ans:3,exp:'**Outliers** may come from measurement error or from real but extreme cases, so they should be investigated rather than automatically deleted. They distort the mean and range much more than the median or IQR.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'A _____ shows the frequency distribution of continuous numerical data as adjacent bars over intervals (bins)',choices:['scatter plot','bar graph','box plot','histogram'],ans:3,exp:'A **histogram** groups numerical data into bins, and bar heights show how many values fall in each bin; the bars touch because the scale is continuous. A bar graph compares separate categories, so its bars are spaced apart.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'A histogram presents _____ data, whereas a bar graph shows _____ data',choices:['categorical / numerical','numerical / categorical','categorical / categorical','nominal / ordinal'],ans:1,exp:'A histogram groups continuous **numerical** data into adjacent bins, whereas a bar graph compares separate **categorical** groups. Reversing them is the common mix-up.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'Slope equals _____ divided by _____',choices:['run / rise','rise / run','Δx / Δy','x / y'],ans:1,exp:'Slope = **rise/run** = Δy/Δx. On a position–time graph the slope is velocity, and on a velocity–time graph it is acceleration.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'The probability of a(n) _____ event does not change based on the outcomes of other events',choices:['dependent','exhaustive','equiprobable','independent'],ans:3,exp:'For **independent** events, P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B); for example, each coin flip is unaffected by earlier ones. Believing a "due" outcome is more likely after a streak is the gambler\'s fallacy.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'The probability of a(n) _____ event changes depending on the outcomes of other events',choices:['independent','dependent','exhaustive','equiprobable'],ans:1,exp:'For **dependent** events, P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B | A); for example, drawing cards without replacement changes the odds for the next draw. Independent events would use simple multiplication.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'_____ describes two or more events that cannot possibly occur together',choices:['Mutually exclusive','Exhaustive','Independent','Conditional'],ans:0,exp:'**Mutually exclusive** events cannot happen together, so P(A and B) = 0 and P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B). They are not independent: if one occurs, the other\'s probability drops to zero.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'When a set of outcomes is _____, there are no other possible outcomes',choices:['exhaustive','mutually exclusive','conditional','independent'],ans:0,exp:'An **exhaustive** set covers every possible outcome, so its probabilities sum to 1. Outcomes that are both mutually exclusive and exhaustive (like heads and tails) are complementary.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'_____ calculate a test statistic and p-value to decide whether the null hypothesis can be rejected',choices:['Confidence intervals','Hypothesis tests','Power analyses','Effect sizes'],ans:1,exp:'**Hypothesis tests** (t-tests, chi-square, ANOVA) compare a test statistic to a known distribution and reject H₀ when p < α. A power analysis is done beforehand to choose the sample size needed to detect an effect.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'The significance of a statistical finding is determined by comparing the _____ to the selected significance level',choices:['confidence interval','effect size','standard error','p-value'],ans:3,exp:'If the **p-value** is less than α (commonly 0.05), the result is statistically significant and H₀ is rejected. The p-value is the probability of results at least this extreme if H₀ were true, not the probability that H₀ is true.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'A _____ gives an estimated range of values likely to include an unknown population parameter',choices:['p-value','confidence interval','standard error','effect size'],ans:1,exp:'A 95% **confidence interval** is the estimate ± about 2 standard errors. If a 95% CI for a difference includes 0 (or for a ratio includes 1), the result is not significant at α = 0.05.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'If a test says someone has a disease but they do not, this is a type _____ error (false positive)',choices:['I','II','III','IV'],ans:0,exp:'A **type I error** (α) rejects a true null hypothesis: a false positive. Its probability equals the significance level, so lowering α reduces type I errors but increases type II errors.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'If a person has a disease but the test does not detect it, this is a type _____ error (false negative)',choices:['I','III','IV','II'],ans:3,exp:'A **type II error** (β) fails to reject a false null hypothesis: a false negative. Power = 1 − β, and power rises with larger sample size and larger effect size.'},
  {s:'Research Methods & Statistics',q:'_____ refers to how closely a study\'s methods, materials and setting resemble real-life situations',choices:['Ecological validity','Construct validity','Internal validity','Statistical validity'],ans:0,exp:'**Ecological validity** asks whether study conditions mirror the real world, so findings apply to everyday settings; it is a component of external validity. Internal validity is about ruling out confounders within the study.'},
];
